1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,700
Plotting distant planets and your own neighborhood.

2
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:09,840
Please proceed to the highlighted route.

3
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:12,780
Maps fueled by adventure.

4
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,400
Older than recorded history.

5
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:20,160
Now, mapmaking on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
A map was there when man first walked on the moon.

7
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:54,480
William Clark drew this map when he charted America's Northwest Passage with Meriwether Lewis.

8
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:57,880
This is the planet Venus.

9
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:00,280
It's also a map.

10
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:06,440
Throughout history, maps have inspired adventure and warned of danger.

11
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:12,300
They've been created as great works of art and to plot horrific carnage.

12
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:15,680
They've also helped us get to where we're going.

13
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:23,500
In addition to geography, the map itself gives you an idea of what the technology was at the time that the map was constructed.

14
00:01:23,501 --> 00:01:29,180
We have maps in the past where there weren't great cartographic techniques and tools,

15
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:32,140
and as a consequence, maps tended to be very simple,

16
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,940
simply because people could not create a complex image.

17
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:39,560
Today, with our advances in computer science and computer graphics,

18
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:43,600
we can create very, very specific displays with great deals of complexity.

19
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:50,640
No place are the technological advances of cartography better demonstrated than in New York City.

20
00:01:50,641 --> 00:01:59,100
During the 20th century, New York generated an incomparable energy stoked by the wonder

21
00:01:59,101 --> 00:02:09,500
of skyscrapers, the power of Wall Street, and the ever-present fear of crime.

22
00:02:09,501 --> 00:02:12,660
Violence tarnished the city's greatness.

23
00:02:12,661 --> 00:02:16,640
In the late 1990s, however, violent crime began to plummet.

24
00:02:16,641 --> 00:02:22,360
A key weapon in New York's war on criminals really wasn't a weapon at all, but a map.

25
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:29,760
A 10-foot-long, interactive, computerized supermap system called ComStat.

26
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:32,639
This is revolutionary.

27
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:37,460
I mean, most police departments in the world are now coming here to copy what we're doing.

28
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:42,400
ComStat is part of the greatest revolution in mapping since Columbus.

29
00:02:42,401 --> 00:02:47,400
technology has taken the map from paper to cyberspace.

30
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,400
Like traditional maps,

31
00:02:50,401 --> 00:02:53,400
Comstat is a geographical representation of an area,

32
00:02:53,401 --> 00:02:57,400
a visual display of streets and landmarks.

33
00:02:57,401 --> 00:03:00,400
But rather than being printed on paper,

34
00:03:00,401 --> 00:03:03,400
it is an electronic projection.

35
00:03:03,401 --> 00:03:08,400
Comstat looms above the NYPD's Command and Control Room,

36
00:03:08,401 --> 00:03:11,400
Crime-Fighting Nerve Center of New York.

37
00:03:11,401 --> 00:03:19,940
The computers that power Comstat actually make it thousands of maps in one.

38
00:03:19,941 --> 00:03:25,900
At the push of a button and a click on a menu screen, Comstat can instantly display a wealth

39
00:03:25,901 --> 00:03:32,460
of updated crime information on virtually every block in the city.

40
00:03:32,461 --> 00:03:36,780
We believe that these maps and the way we use them in this room through our Comstat system

41
00:03:36,781 --> 00:03:40,680
is largely responsible for the decline of crime in New York City.

42
00:03:40,681 --> 00:03:47,280
Numerous icons represent all types of recent crime, including skyscrapers for shootings,

43
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:54,100
dots for robberies, and triangles or kites for narcotic deals.

44
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:59,100
The size of the icon represents the number of incidents.

45
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:02,800
Color indicates the shift on which the crime occurred.

46
00:04:02,801 --> 00:04:07,800
The icons are also the subject of twice weekly meetings,

47
00:04:07,801 --> 00:04:11,800
where the department's 76 precinct commanders are grilled

48
00:04:11,801 --> 00:04:15,800
by the city's police commissioners about crime.

49
00:04:15,801 --> 00:04:17,800
When you see it on a map, they jump right off the page,

50
00:04:17,801 --> 00:04:18,800
they actually speak to you.

51
00:04:18,801 --> 00:04:22,800
You see a cluster of shootings in a two, three block area.

52
00:04:22,801 --> 00:04:24,800
You know instinctively something's going on.

53
00:04:24,801 --> 00:04:27,800
We use this information to question our commanders

54
00:04:27,801 --> 00:04:29,800
and find out who's involved.

55
00:04:29,801 --> 00:04:30,800
Then we ask the narcotics people.

56
00:04:30,801 --> 00:04:33,800
narcotics people, more often than not you find out it's narcotics related.

57
00:04:33,801 --> 00:04:37,800
There's a bit of a turf war going on for control of these corners.

58
00:04:37,801 --> 00:04:40,800
Somebody got shot as they were trying to take over a corner.

59
00:04:40,801 --> 00:04:43,800
The retaliatory shooting is the other one.

60
00:04:43,801 --> 00:04:49,800
Besides plotting crime, the Comstat map provides layer after layer of visual data

61
00:04:49,801 --> 00:04:52,800
related to those crimes.

62
00:04:52,801 --> 00:04:58,800
For instance, Comstat can plot all of New York's banks and automatic teller machines,

63
00:04:58,801 --> 00:05:03,000
locations for criminal activity.

64
00:05:03,001 --> 00:05:08,920
With a click on a menu screen, Comstat displays another important picture, a link between

65
00:05:08,921 --> 00:05:13,740
a rash of burglaries and possible suspects.

66
00:05:13,741 --> 00:05:17,240
This is the border of the 67th and 70th precincts.

67
00:05:17,241 --> 00:05:20,860
The blue diamonds represent residential burglaries.

68
00:05:20,861 --> 00:05:25,160
The green squares that you see surrounding the burglaries are parolees, people on parole

69
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:27,360
from state prison for burglary.

70
00:05:27,361 --> 00:05:32,400
The next logical question is, are any of these people here responsible for all these...

71
00:05:32,401 --> 00:05:36,260
There's a lot of burglaries happening, right, in this little L-shape here.

72
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:38,580
Is the precinct commander aware of it?

73
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:42,460
Have we matched their prints to any of the latent fingerprints we covered at the scenes?

74
00:05:43,020 --> 00:05:45,060
Have we interviewed anybody upon release from prison?

75
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:47,720
These are the obvious questions we ask.

76
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:55,160
Comstat can even display photographs and video of parolees or other suspects in criminal investigations.

77
00:05:55,161 --> 00:06:01,160
While the data contained in Comstat's memory banks can be obtained through written reports,

78
00:06:01,161 --> 00:06:08,160
it is the power of a map, a mosaic of information broken down by geographical reference points

79
00:06:08,161 --> 00:06:10,160
that make it a success.

80
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:18,160
Our precinct commanders will tell you that it's probably the most effective way

81
00:06:18,161 --> 00:06:20,160
to know what's going on in their precinct.

82
00:06:20,161 --> 00:06:25,760
in their precinct really gives us direction and it gives us focus and that's really what maps

83
00:06:25,761 --> 00:06:32,880
are used for maps have been providing direction and focus for thousands perhaps even hundreds of

84
00:06:32,881 --> 00:06:41,120
thousands of years even the oldest maps share a commonality with comstat they're an effort

85
00:06:41,121 --> 00:06:48,640
to communicate in fact maps are one of the first forms of communication no one knows who made the

86
00:06:48,641 --> 00:06:59,020
first map who tried to use a drawing in some way to represent here in relation to there.

87
00:06:59,021 --> 00:07:05,000
We don't know that, but we do know that it probably occurred well before people started

88
00:07:05,001 --> 00:07:06,500
to write.

89
00:07:06,501 --> 00:07:13,480
So when you look back at some of the old maps, you're really looking back into the minds

90
00:07:13,481 --> 00:07:22,560
of people at that time, how they conceived the world they lived in, what they knew, what

91
00:07:22,561 --> 00:07:25,358
they imagined, what they didn't know.

92
00:07:25,359 --> 00:07:29,140
So it's another way of looking into the history of

93
00:07:29,141 --> 00:07:30,481
human development.

94
00:07:30,482 --> 00:07:35,400
Maps are thought to have emerged throughout the prehistoric world,

95
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:40,580
before people could even perceive of civilizations outside of their own.

96
00:07:40,581 --> 00:07:46,820
Maps have been made probably out of any medium you can think of.

97
00:07:46,821 --> 00:07:52,340
There are petroglyphs, paintings on rock that are thought to be map-like.

98
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:59,520
There are all sorts of documented stories of indigenous people making maps in sand

99
00:07:59,521 --> 00:08:05,120
and piling up dirt to form hills and explaining how to get from place to place like that.

100
00:08:05,121 --> 00:08:09,120
Centuries before Europeans explored the Pacific,

101
00:08:09,121 --> 00:08:14,120
Marshall Islanders made maps by lashing sticks and seashells together.

102
00:08:14,121 --> 00:08:18,120
The sticks represented prevailing winds and wave patterns,

103
00:08:18,121 --> 00:08:22,120
while the shells indicated islands.

104
00:08:22,121 --> 00:08:26,120
Footprints showed the route from one village to another

105
00:08:26,121 --> 00:08:32,120
on pre-Columbian Mexican maps that were drawn on dried tree bark.

106
00:08:32,121 --> 00:08:37,280
It is believed that the Babylonians made the first world maps in the 6th century BC,

107
00:08:38,180 --> 00:08:42,420
though the Babylonians' view of the world was limited to the ocean shore.

108
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:50,580
Someone took a stick or some sort of writing instrument

109
00:08:50,581 --> 00:08:56,940
and etched in the clay the Tigris and Euphrates rivers

110
00:08:56,941 --> 00:09:02,780
and in a circular ocean out around there

111
00:09:02,781 --> 00:09:06,720
and then bake the clay to preserve it.

112
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:07,800
So that was a map.

113
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:10,660
By the first century A.D.,

114
00:09:10,661 --> 00:09:14,400
the Roman Empire stretched across Europe and into Africa.

115
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:17,940
At a time when all roads led to Rome,

116
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:20,980
the Romans were the first to make roadmaps.

117
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:25,460
Romans were obviously vitally interested in roads

118
00:09:25,461 --> 00:09:28,560
because they had this huge far-flung empire,

119
00:09:28,940 --> 00:09:32,260
and what tied it together were these wonderful roads that they'd built.

120
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:35,760
One of the largest collections of antiquity maps

121
00:09:35,761 --> 00:09:38,580
resides at Chicago's Newberry Library.

122
00:09:39,480 --> 00:09:42,640
Included in the collection is a 5th century reproduction

123
00:09:42,641 --> 00:09:46,140
of a 1st century A.D. Roman road map.

124
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:49,440
Originally printed on animal skins,

125
00:09:49,441 --> 00:09:52,500
the map is a 15-foot long scroll.

126
00:09:52,501 --> 00:10:01,200
It really shows the whole road network of the empire from Europe all the way to the Near East and all of North Africa

127
00:10:01,201 --> 00:10:08,840
as if it were sort of compressed into this long, narrow band, kind of like modern subway maps.

128
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:14,640
And they have the little notches, little jagged lines on the road indicate a day's march.

129
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:18,660
You could calculate how long it would take you to get from place to place.

130
00:10:18,661 --> 00:10:26,660
Roman roadmaps spanned the gap between primitive prehistoric maps and those that would be based on scientific principles.

131
00:10:26,661 --> 00:10:33,660
Yet, a cultural revolution would suppress scientific map making for hundreds of years.

132
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:45,660
A 1760s English geography game in which wooden maps could be disassembled and put back together gave birth to the jigsaw puzzle.

133
00:10:45,661 --> 00:10:48,160
puzzle.

134
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:51,260
I believe that this nation should commit itself

135
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:55,300
to achieving the goal before this decade is out

136
00:10:55,400 --> 00:10:58,400
of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely

137
00:10:58,500 --> 00:10:59,500
to the Earth.

138
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:02,880
With those historic words, President John F. Kennedy

139
00:11:02,980 --> 00:11:06,640
accelerated what would become the world's second great age

140
00:11:06,740 --> 00:11:07,440
of discovery.

141
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:10,980
Lift off.

142
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:13,720
We have a lift off.

143
00:11:13,721 --> 00:11:19,320
The Apollo missions to the moon were both triumphs of engineering and the human spirit.

144
00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:24,200
God bless all of you, all of you on the good Earth.

145
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:29,640
They also culminated in one of the greatest achievements in the history of cartography,

146
00:11:30,340 --> 00:11:35,200
the precise mapping of an area where no human being had ever stepped foot.

147
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:41,380
Without an accurate map of the moon's crater-popped and boulder-strewn surface,

148
00:11:41,381 --> 00:11:44,680
astronauts Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin

149
00:11:44,681 --> 00:11:48,080
could never have safely landed their lunar module.

150
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:49,440
Contact light.

151
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:51,000
Okay, engine stop.

152
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:55,400
Houston, Tranquility Base here.

153
00:11:55,700 --> 00:11:56,680
The Eagle has landed.

154
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:00,240
Early maps of the moon had been made from Earth

155
00:12:00,241 --> 00:12:04,840
ever since Galileo looked through his primitive telescope in 1610.

156
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:09,420
Of course, the power of telescopes improved exponentially

157
00:12:09,421 --> 00:12:10,920
into the 20th century.

158
00:12:11,380 --> 00:12:18,220
But finding a safe, smooth surface to land a spacecraft on required a closer look.

159
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:24,880
The effort took five years and involved three different series of spacecraft.

160
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:30,660
But the most important mapping data was obtained by five unmanned lunar orbiters,

161
00:12:31,180 --> 00:12:34,400
launched between 1966 and 1968.

162
00:12:36,620 --> 00:12:40,700
Each was equipped with two separate 70-millimeter cameras.

163
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:45,960
One camera took wide-angle images to create a mosaic of the moon's surface.

164
00:12:46,880 --> 00:12:53,560
The other camera, equipped with a telephoto lens, took high-resolution images of potential landing sites.

165
00:12:54,760 --> 00:12:58,440
The five orbiters took a total of nearly 1,500 photographs.

166
00:12:59,260 --> 00:13:04,580
They were electronically beamed back to Earth and constructed into a series of maps.

167
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:06,900
Are you coming down the ladder now?

168
00:13:06,901 --> 00:13:15,980
Small-scale versions of the maps actually traveled with the astronauts who used them to help navigate landing sites.

169
00:13:16,860 --> 00:13:24,300
While the successful lunar landings opened the door to a new range of possibilities for the science of cartography,

170
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:28,980
they also relied on principles that were established centuries earlier.

171
00:13:28,981 --> 00:13:29,763
earlier.

172
00:13:29,764 --> 00:13:38,180
Between the years 125 and 150, a Greek scholar named Ptolemy became the first to apply

173
00:13:38,181 --> 00:13:45,778
science to map making. He recorded his theories in a book titled Geography.

174
00:13:45,779 --> 00:13:47,500
He enunciated the

175
00:13:47,501 --> 00:13:55,680
scientific principles for cartography, the idea of using mapping to scale, the idea of perspective,

176
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:59,480
The rough idea of coordinates, having grids on maps,

177
00:13:59,481 --> 00:14:03,100
all these things that we take for granted in mapping today,

178
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:05,340
Ptolemy enunciated this.

179
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:09,980
Now, he didn't know, and the people at that time didn't have the abilities

180
00:14:09,981 --> 00:14:13,760
to put into practice all of these principles,

181
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:22,320
but they were there on paper or on papyrus to lead the world later on.

182
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:24,740
It would have to be much later.

183
00:14:24,741 --> 00:14:30,120
All of Ptolemy's contributions to cartography would be lost for centuries.

184
00:14:30,121 --> 00:14:36,080
Much of the world's scientific knowledge, including Ptolemy's, was archived at the

185
00:14:36,081 --> 00:14:42,560
library in Alexandria, Egypt. In 391, Christian mobs destroyed the building

186
00:14:42,561 --> 00:14:49,800
and its contents. As the world entered the Dark Ages, mapmaking was assigned to

187
00:14:49,801 --> 00:14:56,236
monks. Suddenly, European maps represented not geography, but theology.

188
00:14:56,237 --> 00:14:58,140
The earliest of these

189
00:14:58,141 --> 00:15:00,343
new maps were called TO maps.

190
00:15:00,344 --> 00:15:04,900
The T symbolized the cross, with the three known continents,

191
00:15:05,100 --> 00:15:08,964
Europe, Asia, and Africa, at each station.

192
00:15:08,965 --> 00:15:12,920
The O was the circular ocean that the monks

193
00:15:12,921 --> 00:15:14,860
believed confined the world.

194
00:15:16,760 --> 00:15:19,820
T.O. maps grew to be far more complex and elaborate,

195
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:22,440
but maintained their biblical perspective.

196
00:15:23,360 --> 00:15:26,220
This is a replica of a map from the year 1240

197
00:15:26,221 --> 00:15:28,740
that was so large it was painted on the wall

198
00:15:28,741 --> 00:15:30,900
of a monastery in Edsdorf, Germany.

199
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:34,680
The original was destroyed in World War II.

200
00:15:35,340 --> 00:15:36,800
Here's the Mediterranean.

201
00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:39,380
If you think of the T.O. map,

202
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:42,160
it's still got the basic T.O. shape,

203
00:15:42,161 --> 00:15:46,460
The top, the Mediterranean here, is the stem of the T,

204
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:52,480
and the bar of the T is the Nile River and the Dardanelles and the Black Sea.

205
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:56,640
The head of Christ is at the top of the map,

206
00:15:57,560 --> 00:16:01,220
his feet at the bottom and his hands at the sides.

207
00:16:02,260 --> 00:16:05,620
He is literally holding the world within his embrace.

208
00:16:05,621 --> 00:16:11,260
The holy city of Jerusalem is centered in an area referred to as

209
00:16:11,261 --> 00:16:15,320
Umbilicus Mundi, the navel of the earth.

210
00:16:16,600 --> 00:16:22,720
Besides the known lands, the map also illustrates the biblical location of the Garden of Eden.

211
00:16:24,700 --> 00:16:30,440
This 15th century Teo map, where the continents are named Ham, Shem, and Japheth,

212
00:16:30,441 --> 00:16:34,380
for the sons of Noah, was one of the last of its kind.

213
00:16:35,620 --> 00:16:39,620
But it's significant for another reason as well.

214
00:16:39,621 --> 00:16:44,620
It was the first known map to be produced on the mechanical printing press,

215
00:16:44,621 --> 00:16:47,620
a new invention that would liberate map making.

216
00:16:47,621 --> 00:16:51,620
Suddenly, maps could be reproduced en masse,

217
00:16:51,621 --> 00:16:54,620
rather than being redrawn by hand.

218
00:16:54,621 --> 00:16:58,620
At the same time, the world had rediscovered Ptolemy.

219
00:16:58,621 --> 00:17:02,620
Prior to the destruction of the Alexandrian Library,

220
00:17:02,621 --> 00:17:07,620
copies of Ptolemy's geography were spirited away by Arab Muslims.

221
00:17:07,621 --> 00:17:12,620
In the late 1300s, they were uncovered in Constantinople,

222
00:17:12,621 --> 00:17:15,620
now Istanbul, Turkey.

223
00:17:15,621 --> 00:17:19,620
Thanks to the printing press, 12 centuries after it was written,

224
00:17:19,621 --> 00:17:24,620
geography was suddenly enjoying mass distribution.

225
00:17:24,621 --> 00:17:27,620
Combined with the liberated thinking of the Renaissance,

226
00:17:27,621 --> 00:17:31,340
of the Renaissance, the old world stood poised

227
00:17:31,341 --> 00:17:34,260
to discover the new world.

228
00:17:34,261 --> 00:17:37,700
Before this Renaissance view, the ocean

229
00:17:37,701 --> 00:17:44,420
was considered the circular body that confined the world.

230
00:17:44,421 --> 00:17:47,180
Some of the savants in Florence began

231
00:17:47,181 --> 00:17:53,260
to hear about the world way out there and read Ptolemy

232
00:17:53,261 --> 00:18:02,920
and began to conceive of the oceans being a medium for communications, not for imprisonment.

233
00:18:03,700 --> 00:18:11,960
It was a liberating concept, and liberation is what it takes to go forward.

234
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:19,840
Ironically, it was an error of Ptolemy's that in 1492 emboldened Christopher Columbus

235
00:18:19,841 --> 00:18:23,780
to cross the Atlantic in search of a short route to Asia.

236
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:29,280
While he understood the spherical nature of the Earth,

237
00:18:29,920 --> 00:18:33,840
Ptolemy had drastically underestimated its circumference.

238
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:39,940
This is a reduced facsimile of a manuscript globe

239
00:18:39,941 --> 00:18:42,520
that was made in Germany in 1492.

240
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:45,580
It's doubtful that Columbus ever saw this globe,

241
00:18:45,720 --> 00:18:47,760
or the original, it's now in Nuremberg,

242
00:18:47,761 --> 00:18:50,940
but he certainly may well have seen something like it.

243
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:58,380
Now, because Ptolemy had very badly underestimated the size of the Earth,

244
00:18:58,381 --> 00:19:00,320
the diameter of the Earth,

245
00:19:00,820 --> 00:19:04,500
and had overestimated the length of the Mediterranean

246
00:19:04,501 --> 00:19:06,700
and the whole length of Asia,

247
00:19:07,260 --> 00:19:13,180
by the time you mapped a too large continent onto a too small globe,

248
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:17,240
you end up with a very short distance between Spain here

249
00:19:17,241 --> 00:19:18,800
and Japan here.

250
00:19:19,520 --> 00:19:21,820
And so I think it demonstrates beautifully

251
00:19:21,821 --> 00:19:25,820
what Columbus obviously had in mind

252
00:19:25,821 --> 00:19:27,820
when he sailed west here from Spain

253
00:19:27,821 --> 00:19:30,100
across the Atlantic,

254
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:32,080
and he struck some islands,

255
00:19:32,520 --> 00:19:34,580
which he quite naturally assumed

256
00:19:34,581 --> 00:19:36,080
were off the mainland of China.

257
00:19:36,180 --> 00:19:37,460
He spent the rest of his career,

258
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:38,660
his next three voyages,

259
00:19:39,180 --> 00:19:40,640
trying to discover the mainland

260
00:19:40,641 --> 00:19:42,460
that he was convinced was there.

261
00:19:43,100 --> 00:19:44,540
Thinking it was Asia,

262
00:19:44,541 --> 00:19:47,480
Columbus most likely landed in the Bahamas.

263
00:19:48,420 --> 00:19:51,300
To add insult to injury, when another explorer,

264
00:19:51,540 --> 00:19:56,220
Amerigo Vespucci, first theorized that Columbus had founded a new continent,

265
00:19:57,180 --> 00:20:00,180
prominent German mapmaker Martin Waldsmuller

266
00:20:00,181 --> 00:20:02,660
named the new land not after Columbus,

267
00:20:03,320 --> 00:20:05,900
but America in honor of Vespucci.

268
00:20:06,600 --> 00:20:07,780
The name stuck.

269
00:20:07,781 --> 00:20:18,700
However, the journeys of Columbus and his contemporaries would lead to a new age of precision mapping

270
00:20:18,701 --> 00:20:25,780
and a white lie that would permeate cartography for the next 500 years.

271
00:20:27,840 --> 00:20:34,240
The magnetic compass is believed to have been invented by the Chinese as early as the 11th century

272
00:20:34,241 --> 00:20:37,420
and introduced to Europe by Marco Polo.

273
00:20:37,780 --> 00:20:50,440
The United States Geological Survey in Reston, Virginia,

274
00:20:50,441 --> 00:20:55,580
is the most advanced and prolific mapmaking entity that the world has ever seen.

275
00:20:55,581 --> 00:21:09,040
The agency runs its own printing plant where every week about 500,000 maps come off the presses.

276
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:20,520
200-pound stacks of maps are positioned with ease as they float on a bed of air

277
00:21:20,521 --> 00:21:25,140
before being trimmed to size on an automatic cutting machine.

278
00:21:25,580 --> 00:21:36,420
Another machine does what so many of us fail to do.

279
00:21:36,940 --> 00:21:40,500
It folds the maps with neat and orderly creases.

280
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:44,120
But there is no machine or technology

281
00:21:44,121 --> 00:21:48,380
that can ever change one truth about mapmaking.

282
00:21:48,381 --> 00:21:53,460
The map is like every other human form, flawed.

283
00:21:53,461 --> 00:21:58,460
You should bear in mind when you look at a map that it is a compromise.

284
00:21:58,461 --> 00:22:04,460
If you're going to have a completely reliable and accurate map of reality,

285
00:22:04,461 --> 00:22:10,460
it would have to be on a scale of one to one, which of course defeats the purpose.

286
00:22:10,461 --> 00:22:13,460
Every map contains a geographical lie,

287
00:22:13,461 --> 00:22:21,460
a compromise that comes from trying to project the geography of a round world onto a flat surface.

288
00:22:21,461 --> 00:22:28,460
The most common and longest-running white lie in mapmaking is called the Greenland problem.

289
00:22:28,461 --> 00:22:35,460
On many world maps, the island of Greenland appears as large as South America.

290
00:22:35,461 --> 00:22:42,460
The reason for this dates back to a time shortly after Columbus's journeys to the New World.

291
00:22:42,461 --> 00:22:50,460
The European invasion of the Americas created a demand for accurate navigational maps.

292
00:22:50,461 --> 00:22:55,460
Yet mapmakers still struggled with precision.

293
00:22:55,461 --> 00:23:01,460
They could draw shapes of land masses based on the observations of explorers.

294
00:23:01,461 --> 00:23:04,460
But their problem was with projection,

295
00:23:04,461 --> 00:23:12,460
an accurate directional representation of a big round world on a small flat piece of paper.

296
00:23:12,461 --> 00:23:22,060
In 1569, a Flemish geographer named Gerardus Mercator used mathematics and his own intuition

297
00:23:22,061 --> 00:23:28,300
to develop a new projection system, one that would not only serve the pilgrims when they

298
00:23:28,301 --> 00:23:36,340
sailed to America 50 years later, but would be one of the most popular projections for the next 500 years.

299
00:23:36,341 --> 00:23:46,820
This is one of the first printed editions of Mercator's world map on his new projection.

300
00:23:47,340 --> 00:23:55,640
It's still basically the rectangular world map that most of us have seen in many a book on Atlas

301
00:23:55,641 --> 00:23:58,720
and probably had hanging up in the walls of our schools.

302
00:23:59,800 --> 00:24:05,880
Mercator's projection made it possible for navigators to draw straight lines between two points.

303
00:24:06,340 --> 00:24:09,680
and use compass bearings to steer between them.

304
00:24:11,600 --> 00:24:16,440
The compromise is that in order to straighten the curving lines of the poles,

305
00:24:16,441 --> 00:24:20,560
the upper and lower landmasses of the map are exaggerated.

306
00:24:22,460 --> 00:24:25,660
The result is the Greenland problem.

307
00:24:27,260 --> 00:24:31,820
The navigational benefits, however, vastly outweigh the distortion.

308
00:24:31,821 --> 00:24:36,620
Mercator's projection is still used by navigators today.

309
00:24:37,620 --> 00:24:41,500
About a hundred years after Mercator devised his projection,

310
00:24:42,380 --> 00:24:45,880
an Italian cartographer named Jean-Dominique Cassini

311
00:24:45,881 --> 00:24:49,420
was employed by the King of France to map the nation.

312
00:24:50,620 --> 00:24:56,440
It was to be done with a level of scientific accuracy never before achieved.

313
00:24:56,441 --> 00:25:03,440
To accomplish this, Cassini devised a system based on geometry.

314
00:25:03,441 --> 00:25:09,440
If you know the length of one side and two of the angles of a triangle,

315
00:25:09,441 --> 00:25:13,440
you can calculate the length of the other two sides.

316
00:25:13,441 --> 00:25:20,440
Thus, taking triangular measurements meant he wouldn't have to travel to every point he mapped.

317
00:25:20,441 --> 00:25:30,220
As innovative as his idea was, mapping France was still an extremely complex and time-consuming task.

318
00:25:30,221 --> 00:25:32,380
It took a hundred years.

319
00:25:33,520 --> 00:25:37,540
Cassini was relieved by his son and his grandson,

320
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:44,960
until finally his great-grandson completed and published the map set in 1745.

321
00:25:44,961 --> 00:25:47,960
Tied together by triangles,

322
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:53,960
this map provides the basis for large-scale topographic mapping.

323
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,980
So the kings of France,

324
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:00,980
just about the time they lost their kingdom to the revolution,

325
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:04,940
finally had a good map of their kingdom.

326
00:26:04,980 --> 00:26:08,960
The French monarchy certainly would not be the first or last

327
00:26:08,980 --> 00:26:12,960
to see their maps altered due to revolution or war.

328
00:26:12,961 --> 00:26:13,661
war.

329
00:26:13,662 --> 00:26:22,520
But thanks to Mercator and Cassini, cartography had a new scientific foundation on which to chart

330
00:26:22,521 --> 00:26:23,468
the future.

331
00:26:23,469 --> 00:26:30,360
In addition to his map projection, Gerardus Mercator was also the first to refer

332
00:26:30,361 --> 00:26:36,920
to a collection of maps as an atlas. Mapmaking will return on Modern Marvel.

333
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:50,580
marvels. America's oldest mapmaker Rand McNally still produces conventional

334
00:27:50,581 --> 00:27:56,840
fold-out and book-style road maps from their headquarters in Skokie, Illinois.

335
00:27:56,841 --> 00:28:05,280
But now long trips can be planned down to the smallest detail on CD-ROMs. These

336
00:28:05,281 --> 00:28:10,200
products make it possible to plan a trip based on a variety of factors including

337
00:28:10,201 --> 00:28:16,900
sightseeing possibilities, road construction, and even traffic congestion patterns.

338
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:23,820
William Rand and Andrew McNally could probably have never dreamt of such possibilities when

339
00:28:23,821 --> 00:28:28,884
they started a small Chicago printing firm in 1856.

340
00:28:28,885 --> 00:28:32,160
Since Chicago was a boom town for

341
00:28:32,161 --> 00:28:38,600
the railroad industry, they began printing train tickets five years prior to the Civil War.

342
00:28:40,200 --> 00:28:45,980
In 1872, they expanded to printing pocket-sized timetable guides for train lines.

343
00:28:46,920 --> 00:28:51,780
It was in these booklets that the first Rand McNally maps appeared.

344
00:28:52,920 --> 00:28:56,420
They were fold-out maps of the nation's train routes.

345
00:28:58,240 --> 00:29:04,340
They listed every populated city, train station, and mail stop on a state-by-state basis.

346
00:29:04,341 --> 00:29:12,180
But in the 1890s, Rand and McNally were capitalizing on another transportation boom, the bicycle.

347
00:29:13,060 --> 00:29:19,120
They began printing bike maps which showed the best bicycle paths in cities all over the country.

348
00:29:20,780 --> 00:29:28,300
Shortly after, the invention of the automobile heralded a brand new type of map, the photo auto guide.

349
00:29:28,301 --> 00:29:32,960
This is one of Rand McNally's photo auto guides, they were called.

350
00:29:32,961 --> 00:29:38,300
It was an early 20th century attempt to help the motorists get around.

351
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:43,900
The guides used a series of photographs to show the routes between specific cities.

352
00:29:44,600 --> 00:29:50,300
They captured landmarks at key intersections and used arrows to indicate the proper direction.

353
00:29:51,880 --> 00:30:01,040
The founder's son, Andrew McNally II, actually photographed the Chicago to Milwaukee route himself while on his honeymoon.

354
00:30:02,040 --> 00:30:05,760
Auto photo guides, though, would soon give way to more familiar maps.

355
00:30:07,080 --> 00:30:11,820
In 1914, Rand McNally was actually asked to make maps

356
00:30:11,821 --> 00:30:14,740
for the first gas stations in Pennsylvania,

357
00:30:15,120 --> 00:30:17,860
and this began the entrance of Rand McNally

358
00:30:17,861 --> 00:30:19,980
into what became the road map industry.

359
00:30:20,700 --> 00:30:24,540
With more American automobile drivers taking to the roads,

360
00:30:24,541 --> 00:30:28,680
Rand McNally was soon printing more complex maps and road atlases.

361
00:30:29,680 --> 00:30:34,780
One of the biggest obstacles to mapping America's roads was that many of them were unmarked,

362
00:30:34,980 --> 00:30:37,340
or only went by local names.

363
00:30:38,380 --> 00:30:45,680
A map draftsman, Martin Brink, devised a solution that resulted in Rand McNally's Blaze Trails series of maps.

364
00:30:47,380 --> 00:30:51,620
The Blaze Trails map assigned a reference number to each highway.

365
00:30:51,621 --> 00:30:59,620
The map contained a key that matched that number to the highway's name and a unique symbol designed by Rand McNally.

366
00:30:59,621 --> 00:31:06,620
The company's own employees painted the symbols on roadside utility poles all across the country.

367
00:31:06,621 --> 00:31:17,620
Twenty years after the first Blaze Trails maps, Rand McNally would face an overwhelming demand for another kind of map.

368
00:31:21,620 --> 00:31:26,740
During World War II, maps were critical to virtually every aspect of battle.

369
00:31:30,020 --> 00:31:36,420
General Dwight Eisenhower used a Mercator projection map to plot a landing point on the coast of France

370
00:31:36,980 --> 00:31:44,020
for what would be one of the most decisive battles of the 20th century, the 1944 D-Day invasion.

371
00:31:45,780 --> 00:31:51,140
Adolf Hitler read a map and wrongly predicted that the attack would come from another flank.

372
00:31:51,620 --> 00:31:59,060
But from the very start of the war, Americans back home eager for news relied not only on

373
00:31:59,061 --> 00:32:02,860
radio but also on maps.

374
00:32:02,861 --> 00:32:10,060
In 1939, within 24 hours of German troops marching into Poland, Rand McNally's supply

375
00:32:10,061 --> 00:32:13,980
of European maps was exhausted.

376
00:32:13,981 --> 00:32:19,420
The company's printing presses worked around the clock to produce World War II maps.

377
00:32:19,421 --> 00:32:22,820
As once independent nations became occupied territories,

378
00:32:23,300 --> 00:32:26,520
cartographers would reflect those changes on the maps.

379
00:32:28,040 --> 00:32:31,560
Map and globe production would continue 24 hours a day

380
00:32:31,561 --> 00:32:33,600
throughout the entire war.

381
00:32:36,140 --> 00:32:39,300
There was a great interest in the public's side

382
00:32:39,301 --> 00:32:43,180
to know what's where, what's the status of the battles,

383
00:32:43,181 --> 00:32:46,780
what's the status of who is in fact now occupying what area.

384
00:32:46,781 --> 00:32:49,540
Besides the major theaters of battle,

385
00:32:49,880 --> 00:32:53,620
Grand McNally's World War II maps also included special sections.

386
00:32:54,440 --> 00:32:58,180
They were devoted to military hardware such as battleships,

387
00:32:59,120 --> 00:33:03,380
the types of uniforms worn by the different armies fighting the war,

388
00:33:03,780 --> 00:33:04,680
and other features.

389
00:33:06,920 --> 00:33:10,700
Maps help families back home feel closer to their loved ones overseas.

390
00:33:10,701 --> 00:33:22,020
Some of the same technology that aided the Allied forces in winning the war would forever alter mapmaking.

391
00:33:23,160 --> 00:33:29,980
Aerial photography was used as early as the Civil War, when pictures were taken out of hot air balloons for reconnaissance.

392
00:33:31,060 --> 00:33:36,280
Wilbur Wright himself took the first aerial photograph from an airplane in 1909.

393
00:33:36,281 --> 00:33:41,520
though it wasn't until World War II that both the aircraft and cameras were

394
00:33:41,521 --> 00:33:46,420
sophisticated enough to actually make maps from the images.

395
00:33:46,421 --> 00:33:51,700
So that was another major revolution. You no longer had to go out and measure

396
00:33:51,701 --> 00:33:55,060
everything on the ground. You could start measuring things in

397
00:33:55,061 --> 00:34:00,380
aerial photographs. The British invention of radar was one of the most important

398
00:34:00,381 --> 00:34:05,560
technological advances to emerge from the war. Without the ability to detect

399
00:34:05,561 --> 00:34:09,020
Hitler's powerful air and naval forces from miles away.

400
00:34:09,800 --> 00:34:12,560
The outcome of the war could have been much different.

401
00:34:14,180 --> 00:34:19,360
In the post-war period, radar was this emerging technology,

402
00:34:20,320 --> 00:34:27,100
and soon people in aviation and in the military and in cartography

403
00:34:27,101 --> 00:34:33,100
began to realize that here was something that enabled you to do something

404
00:34:33,101 --> 00:34:37,940
that hadn't been done before, which is to map the unseen.

405
00:34:40,300 --> 00:34:43,700
Following the war, cartographers from the U.S. military

406
00:34:43,701 --> 00:34:48,100
and Latin American nations embarked on a major mapping expedition

407
00:34:48,101 --> 00:34:50,400
made possible only by radar.

408
00:34:51,760 --> 00:34:56,260
Most of Central and South America's vast forest and mountain ranges

409
00:34:56,261 --> 00:35:00,520
had never been measured by even the most fundamental surveying tools.

410
00:35:00,521 --> 00:35:08,220
But now, ground crews could bounce radar signals off faraway objects and measure the distance

411
00:35:08,221 --> 00:35:10,960
of the signal's rebound.

412
00:35:10,961 --> 00:35:11,798
Teams of U.S.

413
00:35:11,799 --> 00:35:16,760
and Latin American cartographers interviewed forest-dwelling natives to learn

414
00:35:16,761 --> 00:35:24,420
the names of rarely seen and never before mapped geographical areas.

415
00:35:24,421 --> 00:35:30,740
While early radar technology made it possible to map these remote sections of the Earth,

416
00:35:30,741 --> 00:35:37,660
50 years later, radar would advance to mapping other worlds.

417
00:35:37,661 --> 00:35:44,660
A misconception that California was an island was first printed on a map in 1627.

418
00:35:44,661 --> 00:35:51,180
Despite contrary evidence, most map publishers did not correct the mistake until 1784.

419
00:35:54,420 --> 00:35:58,000
This is a simulated flyover of Venus.

420
00:35:59,280 --> 00:36:02,680
It's also an accurate topographical map.

421
00:36:03,640 --> 00:36:11,800
It was created by combining computer graphics with a radar imaging device on board the NASA space probe Magellan.

422
00:36:12,900 --> 00:36:18,360
Venus is Earth's closest neighbor and is similar in size and distance from the Sun.

423
00:36:18,820 --> 00:36:22,340
But it is shrouded by dense, gaseous clouds.

424
00:36:22,341 --> 00:36:27,340
The planet's surface is penetrable only by radar.

425
00:36:27,341 --> 00:36:32,340
Magellan began its orbit around Venus in May of 1990.

426
00:36:32,341 --> 00:36:37,340
In thousands of radar bursts that last less than a second,

427
00:36:37,341 --> 00:36:43,340
Magellan acquired enough information to make detailed images of the Venusian surface.

428
00:36:43,341 --> 00:36:48,340
It transferred the data to NASA's Deep Space Network dishes,

429
00:36:48,341 --> 00:36:54,760
which then transmitted it to the Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena, California.

430
00:36:56,180 --> 00:37:02,660
There, 15-foot-long image strips were combined with computer graphics to create the flyover.

431
00:37:05,440 --> 00:37:08,460
Magellan has revealed the presence of enormous volcanoes,

432
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:12,180
about 30 times more than the number found on Earth.

433
00:37:12,181 --> 00:37:19,180
And then there is Artemis, a 4,000-mile-long lava channel.

434
00:37:19,181 --> 00:37:24,180
Scientists can use this mapping data to learn more about the formation of Venus.

435
00:37:24,181 --> 00:37:28,180
And in the process of getting to know our neighbor,

436
00:37:28,181 --> 00:37:31,180
they might learn more about the Earth's formation.

437
00:37:31,181 --> 00:37:37,180
But back on Earth, maps are being used for more pressing matters.

438
00:37:37,181 --> 00:37:41,780
When a disaster strikes, the first response is to aid the victims.

439
00:37:43,120 --> 00:37:47,720
A secondary response is to find ways to mitigate future damage.

440
00:37:48,660 --> 00:37:51,960
Maps are absolutely necessary to do that,

441
00:37:52,060 --> 00:37:58,000
because what needs to be done, you need to have first a base of where all the populations are now,

442
00:37:58,140 --> 00:38:02,920
where the infrastructure is now, where the roads, where the telecommunications networks are now.

443
00:38:03,600 --> 00:38:07,460
Then you also have to know where the places are most at risk.

444
00:38:07,461 --> 00:38:11,860
Once those are done, you can identify the overlaps, where people are at risk.

445
00:38:12,600 --> 00:38:15,520
This is where disasters are mapped.

446
00:38:16,240 --> 00:38:20,620
The Center for Integration of Natural Disaster Information, or CINDY.

447
00:38:21,140 --> 00:38:26,120
It's located in the Reston, Virginia headquarters of the U.S. Geological Survey.

448
00:38:26,121 --> 00:38:32,520
Cindy was on full alert in 1993 when record rainfalls inundated 31 U.S.

449
00:38:32,521 --> 00:38:38,740
states and three Canadian provinces along the Mississippi floodplain.

450
00:38:39,700 --> 00:38:42,220
Damage was in the billions of dollars.

451
00:38:43,180 --> 00:38:46,600
500 counties were declared disaster areas.

452
00:38:48,060 --> 00:38:52,460
Maps were needed not only to aid rescue crews, but to plan for the future.

453
00:38:52,461 --> 00:38:59,460
USGS aerial photography teams took to the sky to begin mapping the damage.

454
00:38:59,461 --> 00:39:06,460
That aerial photography has been used for identifying places where bridges have been washed out,

455
00:39:06,461 --> 00:39:11,460
places where communities have been isolated, places where rivers have changed course,

456
00:39:11,461 --> 00:39:15,460
and doing damage assessments like that.

457
00:39:15,461 --> 00:39:21,460
In addition, satellite imagery was obtained for a wider view of the damage.

458
00:39:21,461 --> 00:39:26,960
When combined with the aerial photography and other data at the Cindy Lab,

459
00:39:26,961 --> 00:39:32,460
cartographers create computer-generated maps that tell a story.

460
00:39:32,461 --> 00:39:38,460
They portray the Mississippi floodplain prior to the 93 storms.

461
00:39:38,461 --> 00:39:42,460
They show the dramatic swelling of the floodplains

462
00:39:42,461 --> 00:39:47,460
and finally the severe erosion that was caused as the water receded.

463
00:39:47,461 --> 00:39:56,460
These maps will not only assist in restoring the floodplain, but also in the future planning of cities and homes.

464
00:39:56,461 --> 00:40:01,460
These mapping techniques are not only employed during disasters.

465
00:40:01,461 --> 00:40:08,460
The USGS is constantly updating maps to reflect geographical change.

466
00:40:08,461 --> 00:40:15,460
New strips of aerial photography taken from all over the nation are constantly being analyzed

467
00:40:15,461 --> 00:40:20,460
for quality by USGS cartographers.

468
00:40:20,461 --> 00:40:27,460
Next, they're optically corrected for any distortion caused by the landscape, light refraction,

469
00:40:27,461 --> 00:40:29,460
or the camera lens.

470
00:40:29,461 --> 00:40:34,460
The result is a digital orthophoto quadrangle, or DOQ.

471
00:40:34,461 --> 00:40:38,460
It is a measurably correct photo map.

472
00:40:38,461 --> 00:40:42,460
The DOQ is layered over a digital raster graphic,

473
00:40:42,461 --> 00:40:46,460
a computer-scanned image of an older map.

474
00:40:46,461 --> 00:40:50,460
Cartographers can alter the lines on the digital raster graphic

475
00:40:50,461 --> 00:40:54,460
to match geographical changes seen on the DOQ.

476
00:40:58,460 --> 00:41:02,460
The older map will then be reprinted with the corrections intact.

477
00:41:04,460 --> 00:41:11,460
Now, newer technologies are changing the entire concept of what a map can be.

478
00:41:11,461 --> 00:41:18,460
The folded or misfolded maps that for decades have been stored in glove compartments

479
00:41:18,461 --> 00:41:25,460
are giving way to satellite-powered guidance systems installed in automobiles.

480
00:41:25,461 --> 00:41:33,460
More than just a map, it's a sort of co-piloting navigator.

481
00:41:33,461 --> 00:41:39,160
navigator. When a desired address is entered, the system displays a roadmap.

482
00:41:40,940 --> 00:41:43,322
Please proceed to the highlighted route.

483
00:41:43,323 --> 00:41:46,180
It shows the current position of the automobile,

484
00:41:46,181 --> 00:41:51,100
the streets immediately surrounding it, and the direction to proceed toward.

485
00:41:52,560 --> 00:41:58,500
Once the car is moving, the display changes to easy-to-see directional arrows,

486
00:41:58,501 --> 00:42:07,340
while a voice prompt alerts the driver to upcoming turns.

487
00:42:08,400 --> 00:42:12,940
The system can also provide a real-time map of the car's route.

488
00:42:13,740 --> 00:42:18,460
The actual position of the automobile is indicated by a traveling green triangle.

489
00:42:20,560 --> 00:42:27,640
Some products, like the Magellan Navigator, even provide directories to points of interest, such as restaurants.

490
00:42:28,500 --> 00:42:35,500
Select a location category and the system will display nearby possibilities

491
00:42:35,501 --> 00:42:39,500
and then guide you to your choice.

492
00:42:39,501 --> 00:42:45,500
All of this is made possible by 24 orbiting satellites

493
00:42:45,501 --> 00:42:50,500
that comprise the Global Positioning System, or GPS.

494
00:42:50,501 --> 00:42:55,500
The Global Positioning System was a concept developed by the Defense Department

495
00:42:55,501 --> 00:43:01,500
to enable them to better locate their assets during battle.

496
00:43:01,501 --> 00:43:04,000
That is, they wanted to be able to precisely locate

497
00:43:04,001 --> 00:43:07,040
where American troops' facilities and assets were,

498
00:43:07,220 --> 00:43:10,420
and also know where enemy troops' facility assets were.

499
00:43:13,820 --> 00:43:15,980
Developed in the 1970s,

500
00:43:16,300 --> 00:43:20,300
the effectiveness of GPS was most dramatically displayed

501
00:43:20,301 --> 00:43:23,260
in the 1991 Gulf War.

502
00:43:25,500 --> 00:43:34,500
Fighter pilots use GPS to both locate targets and guide their missiles under the secure

503
00:43:34,501 --> 00:43:37,960
cover of darkness.

504
00:43:37,961 --> 00:43:42,680
Commanders on the other side of the world could pinpoint the location of every soldier,

505
00:43:42,681 --> 00:43:51,040
tank and other military vehicles to within 15 feet.

506
00:43:51,041 --> 00:43:56,040
The satellites that power GPS are a high-tech variation of triangulation,

507
00:43:57,240 --> 00:44:00,740
the method used to map France three centuries earlier.

508
00:44:03,140 --> 00:44:06,840
The 24 GPS satellites are constantly orbiting the Earth.

509
00:44:07,440 --> 00:44:10,740
Each one emanates a timing signal to a specific zone,

510
00:44:11,640 --> 00:44:14,040
effectively covering the entire planet.

511
00:44:15,240 --> 00:44:19,840
Atomic clocks time exactly how long the signal takes to travel to the Earth

512
00:44:19,841 --> 00:44:22,520
and rebound back to the satellite.

513
00:44:24,400 --> 00:44:28,140
When a ground receiver such as one installed in an automobile

514
00:44:28,141 --> 00:44:30,280
detects at least three of these signals,

515
00:44:30,800 --> 00:44:34,820
it can analyze their frequency and determine an exact location

516
00:44:34,821 --> 00:44:36,060
based on latitude,

517
00:44:38,160 --> 00:44:38,860
longitude,

518
00:44:39,660 --> 00:44:41,780
and altitude.

519
00:44:43,600 --> 00:44:47,080
Since the computers and GPS receivers are programmed

520
00:44:47,081 --> 00:44:50,400
with corresponding points of specific blocks on Earth.

521
00:44:51,560 --> 00:44:56,320
They can provide the digital directions from one point to another.

522
00:44:58,340 --> 00:45:02,560
Other mapping applications of GPS can save lives.

523
00:45:03,860 --> 00:45:08,000
Emergency response teams use GPS to immediately calculate

524
00:45:08,001 --> 00:45:11,160
the quickest route to any disaster location.

525
00:45:11,161 --> 00:45:20,320
Meanwhile, GPS provides dispatchers with picture-perfect knowledge of where all their emergency vehicles

526
00:45:20,321 --> 00:45:23,500
are located with real-time accuracy.

527
00:45:26,240 --> 00:45:34,120
The maps of today are no longer merely the drawings and ideas of one or two people who

528
00:45:34,121 --> 00:45:35,740
have gone out and explored an area.

529
00:45:35,741 --> 00:45:44,240
They are now the culmination of 1,000 years or 2,000 years of technology that has measured the Earth,

530
00:45:44,760 --> 00:45:49,320
has gathered information about the people on the Earth, the places on the Earth,

531
00:45:49,460 --> 00:45:53,440
and is continuing to gather information about the people and places on the Earth

532
00:45:53,441 --> 00:45:58,800
using everything from aerial photography to satellite means and people still going out

533
00:45:58,801 --> 00:46:03,160
and using the most traditional, the most classic methods of gathering that information.

534
00:46:03,161 --> 00:46:06,500
We haven't lost using any of the technologies.

535
00:46:06,760 --> 00:46:09,260
We've just added to all of those technologies.

